cd /app/projects/tiobe_report_ppt169_20260429 && cat > notes_batch.json << 'NOTES_EOF'
[
  {
    "slide_number": 1,
    "speaker_notes": "Welcome to the TIOBE Index March 2026 analysis. The TIOBE Programming Community Index measures programming language popularity by analyzing search engine queries across 20+ engines including Google, Bing, Yahoo, Wikipedia, Amazon, YouTube, and Baidu. It tracks the number of skilled engineers, courses, and third-party vendors worldwide. The index is updated monthly and has been published since 2002. Today's deck covers the top 20 rankings, biggest movers, historical trends, the Hall of Fame, and strategic implications for technology leaders."
  },
  {
    "slide_number": 2,
    "speaker_notes": "The top 20 rankings reveal several key patterns. Python leads at 21.25% but experienced the largest YoY decline among the top 5, dropping 2.59 percentage points. This suggests market maturation rather than collapse. C surged 2.02 percentage points to reclaim the #2 spot, signaling renewed interest in systems programming. C++ saw the biggest percentage drop at -2.90%, while Java continued its long-term decline, falling to #4. Notable movements include Go dropping from #7 to #16 with a 1.49 percentage point decline — the sharpest drop of any top-20 language. On the positive side, Perl jumped 13 spots from #37 to #24 with a 1.05 percentage point gain, and R climbed 7 spots with a 0.94 point increase. The bottom of the top 20 is highly competitive, with ratings clustered between 0.72% and 0.90%."
  },
  {
    "slide_number": 3,
    "speaker_notes": "The biggest movers tell a story of legacy language resilience and modern language volatility. On the gainers side, Perl's 13-spot jump to #24 with +1.05% is remarkable — this 35-year-old language shows surprising staying power, likely driven by its entrenched position in bioinformatics and system administration. R's 7-spot climb reflects renewed data science interest, possibly as organizations seek alternatives to Python's dominance. Swift re-entered the top 20 at #26, gaining 0.44%. On the losers side, Go's 9-spot drop from #7 to #16 with -1.49% is the most dramatic decline — despite its popularity in cloud-native infrastructure, its TIOBE discussion share has normalized. C++ and Java both posted significant declines, continuing long-term trends. The key insight: older languages are not dying; they are finding renewed niches while some newer languages face hype-cycle corrections."
  },
  {
    "slide_number": 4,
    "speaker_notes": "The historical context spanning 1986 to 2026 reveals fascinating long-term arcs. C is the only language to remain in the top 2 across all nine measurement points — an unmatched 40-year record of consistency. Python's trajectory is the most dramatic: unranked before 1991, it reached #8 by 2006, then surged to #1 after 2018, peaking above 25% before settling at 21.25%. Java dominated from 2005 to 2018, holding #1 with over 25% share, but has steadily declined to under 8% and #4. JavaScript has plateaued around 3-5% for two decades despite the web's explosive growth — this illustrates a critical TIOBE limitation: it measures discussion volume, not code volume. JavaScript is everywhere but generates less 'new' discussion because it's already ubiquitous. Lisp fell from #2 in 1986 to #26 in 2026, while Ada dropped from #3 to #14. The 2002-2026 trend summary shows Python's rise, C's periodic surges, and Java's long decline."
  },
  {
    "slide_number": 5,
    "speaker_notes": "The TIOBE Hall of Fame recognizes the programming language with the highest year-over-year rise in rating. Python is the most decorated language with 7 wins: 2007, 2010, 2018, 2020, 2021, 2024, and 2025. This represents 29% of all years since 2003 — unprecedented dominance. C won 3 times: 2008, 2017, and 2019, showing periodic resurgence aligned with systems programming cycles. Java won twice during its peak dominance: 2005 and 2015. Go won twice in its early growth years: 2009 and 2016. C++ won in 2003 and 2022, and C# won in 2023 following Microsoft's open-source pivot. Notably, no language has ever won back-to-back years — the award captures breakout momentum, not sustained dominance. Python's 7 wins far exceed any other language, confirming its status as the most momentum-driven language in modern computing history."
  },
  {
    "slide_number": 6,
    "speaker_notes": "For talent and hiring strategy: Python's 21.25% share means continued talent availability but increased competition for senior engineers. R's resurgence suggests data science roles may see renewed R demand alongside Python — don't write off R for statistical-heavy positions. Go's decline may actually make hiring easier as hype normalizes. For tech stack decisions: C's surge to #2 signals renewed systems-level interest — embedded, IoT, and performance-critical applications benefit. Rust's steady +0.09% growth makes it the strategic long-term bet for memory-safe systems over declining C++. For strategic context: Python's YoY decline from peak suggests market maturation, not collapse — plan for polyglot environments. Java's 15-year fall from #1 to #4 means enterprises should evaluate modernization paths. Most importantly, remember that TIOBE measures search-engine discussion volume, not actual code volume. Use it as one input alongside GitHub, Stack Overflow, and internal usage metrics. Thank you for your attention — questions welcome."
  }
]
NOTES_EOF
cat notes_batch.json

